[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Sockets: TCP and UDP Sockets in Core Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sockets are fundamental building blocks for network communication in Java. They provide a low-level interface for applications to send and receive data over a network. Java supports two primary types of sockets: TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) and UDP (User Datagram Protocol). Understanding the differences and appropriate use cases for each is crucial for building robust network applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding TCP and UDP"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"TCP is a connection-oriented protocol that provides reliable, ordered, and error-checked delivery of data. It establishes a connection between the client and server before data transfer begins, ensuring that data arrives in the correct sequence and without errors. TCP is suitable for applications where data integrity is paramount, such as web browsing, email, and file transfer."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"UDP, on the other hand, is a connectionless protocol that offers a simpler and faster way to transmit data. It does not guarantee delivery, order, or error checking. UDP is suitable for applications where speed is more important than reliability, such as streaming video, online gaming, and DNS lookups."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"TCP: Connection-oriented, reliable, ordered, error-checked, slower."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"UDP: Connectionless, unreliable, unordered, no error checking, faster."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"TCP Sockets in Java"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a TCP socket in Java, you typically use the `java.net.Socket` and `java.net.ServerSocket` classes. The `ServerSocket` is used by the server to listen for incoming connection requests, while the `Socket` is used by both the client and server to communicate after a connection has been established."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"TCP Server Example"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates a simple TCP server that listens for connections on port 12345 and echoes back any data it receives."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class TCPServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        ServerSocket serverSocket = null;
        try {
            serverSocket = new ServerSocket(12345);
            System.out.println("Server listening on port 12345");

            while (true) {
                Socket clientSocket = serverSocket.accept();
                System.out.println("Client connected: " + clientSocket.getInetAddress().getHostAddress());

                new Thread(() -> {
                    try (PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(clientSocket.getOutputStream(), true);
                         BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(clientSocket.getInputStream()))) {

                        String inputLine;
                        while ((inputLine = in.readLine()) != null) {
                            out.println("Server: " + inputLine);
                            System.out.println("Received from client: " + inputLine);
                        }
                        System.out.println("Client disconnected: " + clientSocket.getInetAddress().getHostAddress());
                    } catch (IOException e) {
                        System.err.println("Error handling client: " + e.getMessage());
                    } finally {
                        try {
                            clientSocket.close();
                        } catch (IOException e) {
                            System.err.println("Error closing client socket: " + e.getMessage());
                        }
                    }
                }).start();
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Could not listen on port 12345: " + e.getMessage());
        } finally {
            if (serverSocket != null) {
                try {
                    serverSocket.close();
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    System.err.println("Error closing server socket: " + e.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"TCP Client Example"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates a simple TCP client that connects to the server on port 12345 and sends a message. It then receives the echoed message from the server."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class TCPClient {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        String serverHostname = "localhost";
        int serverPort = 12345;

        try (Socket socket = new Socket(serverHostname, serverPort);
             PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(socket.getOutputStream(), true);
             BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));
             BufferedReader stdIn = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in))) {

            String userInput;
            System.out.println("Connected to server. Enter messages (or 'exit' to quit):");

            while ((userInput = stdIn.readLine()) != null) {
                out.println(userInput);
                System.out.println("Server: " + in.readLine());
                if (userInput.equals("exit")) {
                    break;
                }
            }

        } catch (UnknownHostException e) {
            System.err.println("Don't know about host: " + serverHostname);
            System.exit(1);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Couldn't get I/O for the connection to: " + serverHostname);
            System.exit(1);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"UDP Sockets in Java"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a UDP socket in Java, you use the `java.net.DatagramSocket` and `java.net.DatagramPacket` classes. The `DatagramSocket` is used to send and receive UDP packets, while the `DatagramPacket` is used to encapsulate the data being sent or received."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"UDP Server Example"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates a simple UDP server that listens for packets on port 54321 and echoes back any data it receives."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class UDPServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        DatagramSocket socket = null;
        try {
            socket = new DatagramSocket(54321);
            System.out.println("UDP Server listening on port 54321");

            byte[] buffer = new byte[256];

            while (true) {
                DatagramPacket packet = new DatagramPacket(buffer, buffer.length);
                socket.receive(packet);

                InetAddress address = packet.getAddress();
                int port = packet.getPort();
                String received = new String(packet.getData(), 0, packet.getLength());
                System.out.println("Received: " + received + " from " + address.getHostAddress() + ":" + port);

                String response = "Server: " + received;
                byte[] responseBuffer = response.getBytes();
                DatagramPacket responsePacket = new DatagramPacket(responseBuffer, responseBuffer.length, address, port);
                socket.send(responsePacket);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        } finally {
            if (socket != null) {
                socket.close();
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"UDP Client Example"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates a simple UDP client that sends a message to the server on port 54321 and receives the echoed message from the server."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class UDPClient {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        String hostname = "localhost";
        int port = 54321;

        try (DatagramSocket socket = new DatagramSocket()) {
            InetAddress address = InetAddress.getByName(hostname);
            byte[] buffer = new byte[256];

            String message = "Hello UDP Server!";
            buffer = message.getBytes();

            DatagramPacket packet = new DatagramPacket(buffer, buffer.length, address, port);
            socket.send(packet);

            packet = new DatagramPacket(new byte[256], 256);
            socket.receive(packet);

            String received = new String(packet.getData(), 0, packet.getLength());
            System.out.println("Received: " + received);

        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Choosing Between TCP and UDP"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"The choice between TCP and UDP depends on the specific requirements of your application. If you need reliable, ordered delivery of data, TCP is the better choice. If you need speed and can tolerate some data loss, UDP is the better choice. Consider the following factors when making your decision:"},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reliability: Does your application require guaranteed delivery of data?"},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Order: Does the order of data packets matter?"},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Speed: Is speed more important than reliability?"},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Overhead: TCP has more overhead than UDP due to its connection-oriented nature."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sockets are a powerful tool for building network applications in Java. By understanding the differences between TCP and UDP, you can choose the right protocol for your specific needs and create robust, efficient network applications. Remember to handle exceptions properly and close sockets when you are finished with them to avoid resource leaks."}]
